{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790923912,"nonce":"rcS35MJwizl2tzM7QOX9k6WxEz8=","title":"A webhook Connection cannot carry a payload mapping, so reaching an incident tool whose API wants a different JSON shape still means writing a service","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0055 §2: \"A webhook Connection may carry a **payload mapping**: a document that renders the request **body and headers** from the envelope for each fact, and may name **where in a 2xx response** the external incident's URL and id are found (a path expression)\". CONTEXT.md, **Payload mapping**: \"It is destination setup, not wording: a failure is a failed delivery, never a fallback. Without one, the plain envelope is sent.\" None of it exists. A webhook Connection's config schema is empty — `\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {}` (`internal/channels/providers/webhook/connection_schema.json`) — and `domain.ChannelConfig` (`internal/channels/domain/ports.go:100-110`) carries no Connection config to the provider at all. The webhook body is the envelope and nothing else, and the response body is \"COUNTED AND DISCARDED\" (`internal/channels/providers/webhook/channel.go:142-145`).\n\n**The demonstration.** incident.io's HTTP alert endpoint (`POST https://api.incident.io/v2/alert_events/http/{alert_source_config_id}`) requires `title` and `status` at the top level (https://docs.incident.io/openapi/tags/alert-events-v2.json). The `oto.notification.v1` envelope has neither key (`internal/channels/render/webhookjson/envelope.go:24-114`), so pointing a webhook channel at that endpoint fails every delivery today, and the only remedy is a bridge service.\n\n**Why it matters.** ADR 0055's Consequences: \"adding a tool is a pull request of one file.\" That sentence is false until a mapping can be stored, rendered, checked and sent; the catalog (its own ticket) has nothing to import onto.\n\n**Where.** Each piece, with the existing machinery it must reuse rather than reinvent:\n\n*Schema.* The mapping is stored with the Connection (`channel_connections.config` or a sibling column; ADR 0055 §6 forbids making it a NotificationTemplate format). It holds a body per fact plus a required default body, so every one of the 20 reasons in `allReasons` (`internal/notification/domain/reason.go:179-199`) renders — ADR 0055 grants no way for a mapping to decline a fact, and this ticket does not invent one. Optional headers, and an optional response path for `external_url` and `external_id`.\n\n*Rendering, with JSON-escaped interpolation and no opt-out.* The language is the Liquid engine NotificationTemplates already use (`github.com/osteele/liquid`, `internal/channels/template/engine.go:144`). The input is the `oto.notification.v1` envelope as a JSON object — the thing the mapping \"renders from\" per §2 — not the NotificationView a template binds. The escaping is `jsonStringContent` (`internal/channels/template/input.go:319`). ⚠️ Not the whole `raw` binder: `newBinder`'s `FormatRaw` case (`input.go:307-308`) escapes for Slack FIRST and then for JSON, which would put `\u0026lt;` into an incident tool's title. A mapping's escape is JSON only. `caseMapped` (`internal/channels/template/filters.go`) already steps around JSON backslash escapes, so case filters stay safe. The rendered result must parse as one JSON object or it is a failure. Header values are checked with `CheckHeaders` (`internal/channels/providers/webhook/headers.go`), including the `X-Oto-*` reservation that 2765f74 adds; `Authorization` stays forbidden, so a vendor's token travels in the Connection's sealed credential, never in a mapping.\n\n*Where it runs.* At claim time, beside `opts.Template` (`internal/notification/service/dispatch.go:599-600`), so the renderer stays a pure function, and BEFORE `PersistRendered` (`dispatch.go:648-649`), so `notification_deliveries.rendered` holds the bytes actually sent — C11. `X-Oto-Signature` then signs the mapped body with no change, because it signs `msg.Payload` (`channel.go:128`). `webhookjson.Validate` (`internal/channels/render/webhookjson/validate.go`) checks for `schema: oto.notification.v1` and must not run that check on a mapped body; the 1 MiB ceiling still applies.\n\n*Failure is a failed delivery, never a fallback.* A mapping that fails at send time is resolved as `dead` with `error_class = config_invalid` (`internal/notification/domain/notification.go:84-86`, terminal), with the rendered attempt and the error on the row, and it is retryable from the audit by `POST /api/v1/deliveries/{id}/retry` (`internal/notification/api/audit.go:353`) once the mapping is fixed. It must NOT fall into the existing render-failure branch's `RenderInvalid` counter and its \"oto could not render a legal payload\" log line (`dispatch.go:603-630`): that counter means \"an oto bug fixed by shipping a new oto\", and a broken mapping is the operator's configuration. It never sends the plain envelope instead.\n\n*Response path.* A path expression over a 2xx JSON response naming `external_url` and `external_id`. ⛔ This reverses part of the rule at `channel.go:283-298` (\"IT MUST NEVER CARRY ONE BYTE THE RECEIVER SENT\"), written because a body snippet made every webhook an SSRF read primitive. That rule still governs `provider_response`, which is unchanged. The extracted values are the only bytes kept: read only from a bounded (`maxResponseBytes`) JSON body, only on a 2xx, `external_url` only if it is an absolute `https` URL under a stated length, `external_id` only if it is printable and under a stated length, anything else dropped as if absent. `domain.DeliverResult` (`ports.go:250-254`) needs a field to carry them. Recording and showing them is 506ff21's work; this ticket extracts and returns them.\n\n*Save-time validation.* Saving renders the mapping against a sample envelope for every one of the 20 reasons, built the way `internal/channels/template/fixtures.go:49` builds a template's fixture corpus (including its hostile-text and zero-value shapes), and refuses with the failing reason named. A mapping that does not render cannot be saved.\n\n*Test send.* Offered from the Connection, going through the same renderer, mapping, validator and transport as a real send, like `internal/channels/service/tester.go:57-66`. `SyntheticView` (`internal/channels/service/synthetic.go:25`) is a firing-alert card; a mapping test needs a fact the operator picks, an Incident fact among them. The UI says plainly that a test send to an incident tool may open a real incident there.\n\n*UI.* Settings → Connections (`web/src/features/settings/ChannelsSection.tsx`): edit the mapping on a webhook Connection, see the per-reason save errors, run the test send.\n\n**Scope.** large.\n\n**Done when.** A webhook Connection with no mapping sends the plain envelope byte-for-byte as today (the webhookjson goldens unchanged); one with a mapping sends the rendered body and headers, signed; a test proves a label holding `\"`, `\\`, a newline and `\u003c/script\u003e` lands as a valid JSON string and never as structure; a test proves a mapping that renders invalid JSON at send time leaves the delivery `dead`/`config_invalid` with nothing sent and the plain envelope never sent, and that `RenderInvalid` did not move; a mapping failing any one of the 20 reasons cannot be saved, and the 422 names that reason; the response path extracts `external_url`/`external_id` from a 2xx JSON body and drops a non-https URL, an oversized value and a non-JSON body without failing the delivery; `provider_response` still carries no receiver byte; the test send works from Settings → Connections.\n\n**Blocked by.** 2765f74 — the `X-Oto-*` header reservation a mapping's headers are checked against.\n\n**Mode.** AFK. Source: ADR 0055 §2 and §6, ticket-filing session 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}